welcome to the daily climate show coming  up  eu countries warn of moving back to  coal-fired power plants to tackle  russian energy crisis  [Music]  the u.n moves a major biodiversity  summit from china to canada  and heavy rainfall in china and other  parts of asia forces thousands of people  out of their homes  [Music]  hello and welcome to the daily climate  show where we report on the impact of  climate change hear from those most  affected and track the changes that are  responsible for those impacts on the  planet well first last year's government  around the world committed to help  tackle climate change and a big part of  that was phasing out the use of dirty  fossil fuels as an energy source but the  war in ukraine has exposed a reliance on  russian gas and as that supply gets ever  more restricted it's caused an energy  crisis and soaring prices for households  now some countries in the european union  a warning that they'll need to move to  emergency measures and shift their  reliance back to coal so let's take a  look shall we at what's being suggested  so austria's government has agreed to  convert a gas-fired power plant to use  coal in the face of an energy emergency  germany announced on sunday its latest  plant-based gas storage levels and said  it could restart coal-fired power plants  that it had aimed to phase out now the  dutch government has announced that  coal-fired energy plants in the  netherlands will be allowed to increase  their production as part of efforts to  reduce reliance on russian gas and italy  says it may declare a heightened state  of alert on gas this coming week if  russia continues to curb its supplies  which could ramp up the production at  coal-fired plants well i'm joined now by  our correspondent charlotte lomas so  what we're hearing here charlotte is  there is a reluctance to to leaning on  coal in the eu for the short term at  least there is a reluctance but this is  the reality this is the challenge that  is facing european ministers with russia  really squeezing its grip on european  gas supplies many ministers across  europe have now said that they have no  choice but to reconsider coal power  plants as an alternative energy supply  just when  many countries were hoping to to phase  that out and as you mentioned uh coal is  uh one of the greenhouse gases that just  produce uh the most uh pollution it's  known as a dirty energy source so it  will be a reluctant decision one  minister's um said has been a painful  decision um but that is the challenge  that they're facing it could be a stop  gap as you mentioned the big question is  whether this will delay that transition  away from the use of fossil fuels okay  charlotte thank you very much for that  well let's take a look at the day's  other climate news and to china now  where hundreds of thousands of people  have been forced to leave their homes as  the south of the country experiences its  heaviest rainfall since  1961 in shaogon city the flood alerts  was raised to its highest level as  authorities asked residents living along  the riverbanks to move to higher ground  while the summer rainy season happens  every year in china climate change can  bring heavier and more frequent  downpours  well all of this comes as bangladesh and  north eastern india continue to deal  with extreme flooding authorities say  they're struggling to reach  more than nine and a half million people  who have been stranded with little food  and water and the unusually heavy  monsoon rains have killed dozens of  people over the past two weeks  and more than a hundred temperature  records could be broken in the united  states this week as a heatwave continues  and most of the population will see  temperatures over 30 degrees celsius  with the southern and eastern regions  seeing the hottest temperatures  meanwhile firefighters in new jersey  have continued to battle a forest fire  which has consumed over 7 000 acres now  it's all down to dry and windy  conditions meaning the initially small  fire began to spread very quickly  now the average price for petrol and  diesel has risen to a new high according  to the rac the average price of unleaded  is now  188.74 pence per litre and while the  price of diesel is at  196.36 pence per liter an rac spokesman  said that while the price of diesel is  likely to keep rising they expect  retailers to start reducing the price of  petrol in response to a fall in  wholesale costs  now chances are you would have heard of  the cop summits that happened and  focusing on climate like the one held in  glasgow last november well the united  nations  also holds another cop meaning  conference of parties on biodiversity  and this year it's just been announced  that the summits will be moved from  china to canada because of the chinese  zero coved policy well here too tell me  a little bit more about the summit  itself um and that last minute change of  location is mark goff he's the ceo of  the capitals coalition which has been  involved in uh meetings leading up to  cop 15. thanks for joining us  mark so this delay of two years the team  will have so much to get through but how  much of that will you be able to achieve  now  well i think it's really important that  we do move this to canada and that's  been key because we've already lost two  years of this 10-year plan that we were  trying to set out so it's really  important we start moving towards  getting to some solutions  the climate cop is obviously extremely  important but biodiversity we've already  lost 83 of our wild animals 50 of plants  there's another million species that are  under threat so this is the basis of all  of the dependency that we have in life  and it's really essential that we get  this bit right  and what big aim do you want to achieve  i've been reading around this story and  there are a few people that say you know  that two-year delay lost momentum in in  really making a change  um it's lost momentum on getting to the  final decision but it's allowed a lot  more countries to actually start having  discussions around what is important and  we're talking about something here which  is very  difficult to get your head around it's  about a local issue it affects people  locally unlike climate which is a big  issue that one ton of carbon is the same  all around the world what we're talking  about here is something which is local  to people what we're really aiming for  is a global goal agreement across all of  the countries for a nature positive but  also equitable and climate neutral  approach and by nature positive we mean  there's more nature by 2030 than there  is now indeed what would be your message  mark to world leaders because i suppose  the success of um cop 26 was that big  world leaders turned up and were  involved and this will be after cop 27  in egypt it also clashes with the world  cup what would you say to leaders whose  attention might be elsewhere at this  time  well one thing um world cups are going  to happen again this is only a once in a  decade at least discussion so maybe give  up the world cup tickets to come to this  one  we do need to get the world leaders  there we do need to get the commitments  but we have started seeing this the  connection between the climate cop where  92 percent of the nationally determined  contributions the country set included  nature so these things aren't separate  they're all interconnected so we have to  get this one right world leaders have to  be there this one to get the overarching  agreement around the world  and very briefly mark what are your  thoughts on that move then we said it at  the beginning from china to canada  well like i say i mean i'm really  pleased that the uh chinese government  has agreed to move it to canada so we  can get on and do this um covert has  been such a disruption to so many people  around the world in so many ways not  just because of this biodiversity  conference but we